function topicDeepDive(p){
  return `<section class="section"><h2>Deep dive: ${esc(p.angle)}</h2><p>Use this section as a working vocabulary. The same word can mean different things in a sales conversation, a buyer’s order, and a lending disclosure. The disciplined buyer asks which document controls, who receives the money, and what changes if the item is removed.</p>${p.topics.map(t=>`<div class="fee-card"><h3>${esc(t)}: what to inspect</h3><p>Start by locating <b>${esc(t)}</b> in the paperwork rather than relying on the label used in a conversation. Check whether the amount is a fixed dollar charge, a percentage, a recurring amount, or a financed lump sum. Then ask whether it changes the vehicle price, the amount financed, the finance charge, the protection you receive, or only the convenience of completing the transaction. A useful explanation identifies the provider, the benefit, the term, the exclusions, and the cancellation or refund rule.</p><p>Next, compare the item with an independent benchmark. For a government charge, use the relevant state or county agency. For a loan term, compare a bank or credit-union preapproval. For an insurance-like or service product, ask for a quote and the full contract from another provider. For a dealer fee, compare the complete out-the-door price from another dealer instead of assuming the fee is the only negotiable line.</p><div class="protip"><strong>Question to use:</strong> “If I remove ${esc(t)}, what exact numbers change in the out-the-door total, amount financed, APR, payment, and total of payments?”</div></div>`).join('')}<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>What it answers</th><th>What to reconcile</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Advertisement or worksheet</td><td>What was offered</td><td>Stock number, eligibility, expiration, included products</td></tr><tr><td>Buyer’s order</td><td>What is being purchased</td><td>Vehicle price, fees, trade, taxes, total due</td></tr><tr><td>Retail installment contract</td><td>What is being financed</td><td>APR, amount financed, finance charge, payments, term</td></tr><tr><td>Product agreement</td><td>What an add-on promises</td><td>Coverage, exclusions, claims, cancellation, refund</td></tr><tr><td>Delivery paperwork</td><td>What changed at handoff</td><td>Condition, keys, mileage, final copies, open promises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Questions that reveal the real deal</h3><p>Ask the questions in writing when possible. “Which of these charges are government charges?” separates pass-through amounts from dealer revenue. “Which products are optional?” tests whether the menu is being presented accurately. “What is the amount financed without each product?” shows whether the product is being paid for with borrowed money. “What is the total of payments if I keep the loan to maturity?” exposes the long-term cost.</p><p>For financing, ask “What is the lender’s buy rate?” and “Is the APR higher than that rate because of dealer compensation?” You may not receive every internal number, but the question establishes that you are comparing credit as a price. For a trade, ask for the payoff and allowance separately. Combining them can make negative equity disappear into the new loan.</p><p>For service products, ask “Who authorizes the repair?” “Where can I take the car?” “What is excluded?” and “How is the refund calculated if I cancel or pay off early?” A product with a broad sales description may have a narrow contract. For GAP, ask about maximum benefit, term, exclusions, and whether your existing auto insurer offers a different price.</p><h3>Useful negotiation language</h3><div class="callout"><p>“Please email me the out-the-door price for this VIN with every optional product removed. I will choose products separately after I review the contracts.”</p><p>“I am comparing loan offers by APR, amount financed, finance charge, term, and total of payments. Please show those numbers without changing the vehicle price.”</p><p>“I do not agree to this item as written. Remove it and provide a corrected buyer’s order before we discuss signing.”</p></div></section>`;
}

function extendedFieldGuide(p){
  const t=p.topics;
  const sections=[
    ['The first quote is a baseline, not a promise',`A first worksheet is useful because it creates a time-stamped baseline for ${t[0]}. Treat it as a starting point to verify, not as a final contract. Capture the vehicle identification number or stock number, the mileage, the advertised selling price, the date, and the conditions attached to rebates. If a number is only available after a credit application, ask whether the quote is conditional on financing, trade-in, military status, membership, or other eligibility. A clean baseline lets you identify what changed later and whether the change was explained before you agreed.`],
    ['Read the transaction in layers',`The most reliable review separates the transaction into the vehicle, required government charges, dealer charges, optional products, trade equity, and credit. This is especially important when ${t[1]} appears in a menu with several unrelated products. Classify the line first, then evaluate price and value. A line can be legal but still optional, negotiable, duplicative, or a poor fit for your situation. Classification prevents the conversation from collapsing into an argument about whether one label sounds normal.`],
    ['Follow the arithmetic trail',`Build a hand calculation for ${t[2]} rather than trusting a rounded payment. Start with the agreed vehicle price, subtract discounts that you actually qualify for, add the charges listed in the buyer’s order, apply taxes and official fees, account for trade allowance and payoff, subtract cash down, and add only the products you selected. Then compare the result with the amount financed. Differences deserve a written explanation. A calculator cannot tell you whether a fee is fair, but it can show you exactly where the obligation grew.`],
    ['Compare like with like',`A comparison is useful only when the inputs match. When evaluating ${t[3]}, compare the same vehicle or a genuinely comparable vehicle, the same trade assumptions, the same cash down, the same loan term, and the same optional products. Otherwise a lower payment may simply reflect a longer term or a larger down payment. Ask each seller for an out-the-door total and each lender for APR, amount financed, finance charge, payment schedule, and total of payments. Put the offers in one table before choosing.`],
    ['Test the optional-product decision',`Optional products connected to ${t[4]} should be evaluated as separate purchases. Read the product’s term, benefit trigger, exclusions, deductible, administrator, repair network, claims procedure, and cancellation formula. Ask whether the product overlaps with the manufacturer warranty, your auto insurance, a roadside plan, or a benefit you already receive. If you still want it, ask for the price with cash and the price when financed. The financing difference is part of the product’s real cost.`],
    ['Stress-test the loan',`For a deal involving ${t[5]}, run three simple tests. First, ask what happens if you pay the loan for the full term. Second, ask what happens if you sell or total the vehicle early. Third, ask what happens if your income changes and a payment is late. Look for prepayment penalties, late charges, insurance requirements, default language, negative-equity carryover, and products that expire before the loan does. These questions do not predict your future; they reveal the conditions that control your options.`],
    ['Check the state and agency context',`State rules matter when ${t[0]} or ${t[1]} is regulated as a dealer charge, title charge, tax, insurance product, or lending term. Identify the state where the vehicle is sold and titled, then use the official motor-vehicle, attorney-general, insurance, or financial regulator source for current rules. Do not treat an online fee table as legal advice. A fee cap may limit one charge while allowing a dealer to negotiate the total price in another way. Record the source and date you relied on.`],
    ['Document the conversation',`Good evidence is calm and precise. Save the advertisement, worksheet, quote, emails, text messages, buyer’s order, retail installment contract, add-on agreements, and delivery receipt. Write down who made a promise about ${t[2]}, what was promised, and when. If the final paperwork differs, identify the exact two lines and ask for correction. Avoid editing the original file; make a copy for markup. A clean record helps a lender, regulator, attorney, or dealer manager understand the issue without reconstructing the entire conversation.`],
    ['Negotiate the total, then the terms',`A practical negotiation around ${t[3]} starts with a written total rather than a target payment. Say that you are comparing the complete out-the-door price, then separately comparing financing and optional products. If a fee cannot be removed, ask whether the vehicle price can be adjusted so the total remains competitive. If the APR is not competitive, show your preapproval and ask for a matching structure. You do not need to prove that every fee is improper; you only need a comparable alternative and a clear walk-away number.`],
    ['Review the final packet at delivery',`Before you leave, compare the signed packet with the last quote line by line. Confirm the VIN, mileage, vehicle price, discounts, trade allowance, payoff, cash down, selected products, APR, term, payment, amount financed, and total of payments. Confirm who will receive the first payment and when it is due. If ${t[4]} is canceled or declined, make sure the box or signature shows that choice. If financing is conditional, identify the exact condition and deadline. Take every signed page home; a promise to email it later is not a substitute.`],
    ['What changes after signing',`Some questions about ${t[5]} arise after the first statement arrives. Compare the statement with the signed contract, including the account number, payment amount, due date, fees, and any financed product. If a cancellation or refund was requested, ask how it was applied and whether it reduced the principal. If the payment differs, request a written account history and calculation. Do not stop paying a loan based only on a dispute; get advice about the contract and preserve your complaint record while you seek a correction.`],
    ['When an expert review is worthwhile',`An educational guide can help you identify questions, but some situations merit professional help. Consider a qualified attorney or consumer advocate when documents conflict, the dealer refuses to provide a copy, the amount financed is materially different, the vehicle was delivered on uncertain terms, a cancellation is denied, or you suspect discrimination or deceptive conduct. Bring the complete packet and a one-page timeline. A focused packet makes professional review more efficient and reduces the risk that an important document is missed.`]
  ];
  const cards=sections.map((s,i)=>`<div class="fee-card"><h3>${s[0]}</h3><p>${s[1]}</p><p><b>Apply it here:</b> For this guide, use the same test on <strong>${t[i%t.length]}</strong> and compare the result with <strong>${t[(i+1)%t.length]}</strong>. The purpose is to make the relationship visible rather than treating either line in isolation.</p></div>`).join('');
  return `<section class="section"><h2>Field guide: making the numbers defensible</h2><p>Authority content should help a reader make a decision, not just recognize a term. The following review sequence turns ${esc(p.angle)} into evidence a buyer can explain, compare, and act on.</p>${cards}<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>What to ask for</th><th>Decision signal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The price changes in the finance office</td><td>Last quote, buyer’s order, and a clean version without optional products</td><td>Pause until the difference is explained and corrected</td></tr><tr><td>The payment is lower than expected</td><td>APR, term, amount financed, and total of payments</td><td>Lower payment may mean a longer or more expensive loan</td></tr><tr><td>An add-on is called required</td><td>Written lender, legal, or contract requirement</td><td>Decline or renegotiate if no requirement is shown</td></tr><tr><td>Delivery is conditional</td><td>Condition, deadline, financing status, and return instructions</td><td>Do not accept new terms without a line-by-line comparison</td></tr><tr><td>A refund is promised</td><td>Cancellation form, refund formula, and posting timeline</td><td>Keep proof and verify the loan balance or payment changes</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>`;
}

function decisionLab(p){
  const t=p.topics;
  const entries=[
    ['Decision 1: keep, remove, or replace',`When ${t[0]} appears in your paperwork, put it into one of three buckets: keep because the benefit is clear and independently priced; remove because it is optional, duplicative, or not useful to you; or replace because another provider offers a better version. This avoids the false choice between accepting the dealer’s product and rejecting the entire transaction. Write the reason next to the line so the decision remains clear when the finance office presents several products at once.`],
    ['Decision 2: compare today versus over the term',`The price of ${t[1]} is not always the same as the amount you repay for it. If the product is financed, interest and a longer term add to the obligation. Ask for the cash price, the financed amount, and the difference in total of payments. If a product ends after three years but the loan lasts seven, you may continue paying after the stated benefit period. The time mismatch should be visible before you sign.`],
    ['Decision 3: distinguish uncertainty from risk',`A line involving ${t[2]} may be unclear without being improper, while a clear clause may still create a risk you do not want. Mark uncertainty when you need an explanation or a missing document. Mark risk when the term is understood but could cost you more, restrict your repair options, or change what happens after default. This distinction helps you ask precise questions instead of making a broad accusation that may distract from the correction you need.`],
    ['Decision 4: use a written walk-away number',`Set a maximum out-the-door total and a maximum acceptable financing cost before the meeting becomes tiring. Include taxes, dealer charges, selected products, negative equity, APR, term, and total of payments in the calculation. If ${t[3]} pushes the deal over that number, ask for a corrected quote or leave. A walk-away number is not a demand that a dealer match your budget; it is a boundary that keeps fatigue from becoming a financial decision.`],
    ['Decision 5: check what the document actually controls',`Sales worksheets, menu sheets, advertisements, buyer’s orders, retail installment contracts, service contracts, GAP certificates, and delivery receipts do different jobs. When ${t[4]} is disputed, identify which document contains the operative term and whether another document contradicts it. Keep the documents together but do not treat a summary as a substitute for the full agreement. If a salesperson’s promise is absent, ask whether the promise can be added or corrected in writing before signing.`],
    ['Decision 6: plan the post-sale checkpoint',`Schedule a review after the first lender statement and after any cancellation request. Compare the posted principal, payment, due date, and product balances with the signed paperwork. This is especially useful for ${t[5]}, where a refund or cancellation may be processed by a separate administrator. If something is wrong, contact the lender or administrator promptly, preserve the original documents, and create a dated record of every response.`],
    ['Decision 7: prepare a regulator-ready summary',`If the issue cannot be resolved, reduce it to one page: what was offered, what was signed, what changed, what you requested, and what response you received. Attach the relevant pages and highlight only the numbers tied to ${t[6%t.length]}. A regulator or attorney can act more efficiently when the facts are organized. Keep opinions separate from documents and state what you want corrected, refunded, or explained.`],
    ['Decision 8: use AI as a second set of eyes',`HiddenFeeAI can help organize a quote, purchase agreement, financing contract, or product agreement around ${t[7%t.length]}. Use the output to locate lines, compare documents, and draft questions; do not treat an automated flag as a final legal or financial conclusion. The strongest workflow combines machine-assisted triage with the buyer’s own context, official sources, and professional review when the stakes are high.`],
    ['Decision 9: protect the comparison after the meeting',`Deal terms can blur after a long meeting. Immediately save the final PDF, photograph the signed pages, and label the files with the date and document type. Record whether ${t[0]} was accepted, declined, or changed. If a dealer sends a revised document, keep both versions and note the differences. This small habit prevents a later conversation from turning into a disagreement about which version was actually approved.`],
    ['Decision 10: teach the next buyer',`A useful authority guide should leave you with a method you can explain to someone else. Tell the next buyer to request the out-the-door total, separate optional products, compare APR and total of payments, verify every signature and blank, and keep the packet. When the topic is ${t[1]}, share the exact question that exposed the cost rather than only the result. Better questions improve transparency for everyone in the transaction.`],
    ['Decision 11: separate a correction from a concession',`A corrected document fixes a mismatch between what was agreed and what was written. A concession changes the price or term as a new bargain. Keep those ideas separate when discussing ${t[2]}. Ask first for the document to match the agreement; then decide whether the corrected total is competitive. This keeps the record clear and avoids accepting a discount in exchange for leaving an inaccurate line untouched.`],
    ['Decision 12: close the loop in plain language',`At the end of a review, summarize the decision in one paragraph: what you accepted, what you declined, what remains unanswered, and what document will prove the final terms. For ${t[3]}, include the price, APR, term, amount financed, and product selections. Send that summary to yourself or the dealer contact. A clear closing note reduces memory gaps and gives you a reference if a later statement or revised document raises a question.`]
  ];
  return `<section class="section"><h2>Decision lab: apply the guide to a real deal</h2><p>Use these checkpoints after you have read the definitions and before you commit. They are designed for follow-up questions a buyer is likely to ask about ${esc(p.angle)}.</p>${entries.map(e=>`<div class="fee-card"><h3>${e[0]}</h3><p>${e[1]}</p></div>`).join('')}</section>`;
}

function schema(p, faq){
  const faqSchema = faqQuestions(p).map(q=>({"@type":"Question",name:q,acceptedAnswer:{"@type":"Answer",text:`Read the answer in the expandable FAQ section of ${p.title}.`}}));
  return `<script type="application/ld+json">${JSON.stringify({"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article",headline:p.title,description:p.short,author:{"@type":"Organization",name:'DetectHiddenFees Research Team'},publisher:{"@type":"Organization",name:'DetectHiddenFees',url:site},datePublished:today,dateModified:today,mainEntityOfPage:{"@type":"WebPage", "@id":`${site}/${p.slug}`},about:p.topics})}</script><script type="application/ld+json">${JSON.stringify({"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage",mainEntity:faqSchema})}</script><script type="application/ld+json">${JSON.stringify({"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList",itemListElement:[{"@type":"ListItem",position:1,name:'Home',item:site},{"@type":"ListItem",position:2,name:'Car Dealer Fee Authority Hub',item:`${site}/car-dealer-fees`},{"@type":"ListItem",position:3,name:p.title,item:`${site}/${p.slug}`}]})}</script><script type="application/ld+json">${JSON.stringify({"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"SpeakableSpecification",cssSelector:['.answer-first','.key-takeaways']})}</script>`;
}

function svg(p){ return `<div class="diagram" role="img" aria-label="${esc(p.title)} visual explainer"><svg viewBox="0 0 960 230" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><linearGradient id="g" x1="0" x2="1"><stop stop-color="#38bdf8"/><stop offset="1" stop-color="#a78bfa"/></linearGradient></defs><rect width="960" height="230" rx="24" fill="#0b1730"/><path d="M90 115H850" stroke="#31466f" stroke-width="4" stroke-dasharray="10 10"/><g font-family="Inter,Arial" text-anchor="middle"><g><rect x="38" y="64" width="150" height="102" rx="16" fill="#11264b" stroke="#38bdf8"/><text x="113" y="95" fill="#bae6fd" font-size="16" font-weight="700">START</text><text x="113" y="125" fill="white" font-size="15">Sticker price</text><text x="113" y="148" fill="#94a3b8" font-size="12">written quote</text></g><g><rect x="242" y="64" width="150" height="102" rx="16" fill="#18244d" stroke="#818cf8"/><text x="317" y="95" fill="#c7d2fe" font-size="16" font-weight="700">CHECK</text><text x="317" y="125" fill="white" font-size="15">Fees + add-ons</text><text x="317" y="148" fill="#94a3b8" font-size="12">required or optional?</text></g><g><rect x="446" y="64" width="150" height="102" rx="16" fill="#252050" stroke="#a78bfa"/><text x="521" y="95" fill="#ddd6fe" font-size="16" font-weight="700">COMPARE</text><text x="521" y="125" fill="white" font-size="15">APR + total</text><text x="521" y="148" fill="#94a3b8" font-size="12">not payment alone</text></g><g><rect x="650" y="64" width="272" height="102" rx="16" fill="url(#g)"/><text x="786" y="95" fill="#061126" font-size="16" font-weight="800">DECIDE</text><text x="786" y="125" fill="#061126" font-size="15" font-weight="700">Ask, edit, or walk</text><text x="786" y="148" fill="#061126" font-size="12">then sign the final copy</text></g></g></svg><p class="diagram-caption">The buyer’s decision path: move from a headline price to a verified total.</p></div>`; }

