function body(p, faqHtml){
  const other = pages.filter(x=>x.slug!==p.slug).slice(0,6);
  const deep = topicDeepDive(p) + extendedFieldGuide(p) + decisionLab(p);
  const extra = `<section class="section"><h2>Before you leave the dealership</h2><p>Do one final read aloud of the numbers. Say the vehicle price, the trade allowance, the trade payoff, the cash down, the selected products, the APR, the term, the payment, the amount financed, and the total of payments. Hearing the sequence can expose a number that was assumed rather than agreed. If someone says the number is “close enough,” ask for a corrected document.</p><p>Check the dates and signatures, including any co-buyer or co-signer. Confirm that the seller, lender, product administrator, and buyer names are spelled correctly. Make sure the vehicle identification number and odometer reading are consistent. Review whether the contract includes a final payment, deferred payment, prepayment penalty, late-fee rule, insurance requirement, or electronic payment authorization that was not discussed.</p><p>Ask where and how to cancel an optional product, how refunds are calculated, and whether a refund reduces the loan balance or is sent directly to you. Ask the lender how payments are applied and where to find the account number. If delivery is conditional, get the condition and deadline in writing and do not assume the dealer’s call means you must accept a more expensive loan.</p><p>Finally, take the complete signed packet with you. Do not rely on a promise that it will be emailed later. Photograph the odometer, the vehicle condition, and any temporary tag or delivery document. Keep a simple file with the purchase date, first payment date, lender contact, insurance proof, warranty terms, and all cancellation requests. Good records preserve your options if a correction is needed.</p><div class="callout warning"><strong>Last question:</strong> “Is there anything I am being asked to sign, pay, or insure that is not shown in the written total or explained in the contracts I am taking home?”</div></section>`;
  return `${sharedHeader}<main id="main-content" class="article-page"><nav class="phase2-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="phase2-separator" aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/car-dealer-fees">Car Dealer Fee Authority Hub</a><span class="phase2-separator" aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">${esc(p.title)}</span></nav><div class="wrap"><div class="hero"><div class="badge">${p.tag}</div><h1>${p.title}</h1><p class="hero-sub lede">${p.short} This guide explains ${p.angle} with a human-first review method, examples, negotiation language, and source links you can use at the dealership.</p><div class="hero-trust"><span>Human-first research</span><span>Citation-ready</span><span>Updated ${today}</span><span>Not legal or financial advice</span></div></div><div class="jump"><a href="#summary">Summary</a><a href="#review">Review process</a><a href="#red-flags">Red flags</a><a href="#faq">FAQs</a><a href="#checklist">Printable checklist</a></div><div class="grid"><article class="article">${deep}
<section id="summary" class="section"><h2>Executive summary</h2><div class="answer-first"><strong>Short answer:</strong> The safest way to evaluate ${p.angle} is to separate the vehicle price, government charges, dealer charges, optional products, trade equity, and financing terms. A monthly payment can hide the actual cost; the complete written out-the-door total and Truth in Lending disclosures are the useful comparison points.</div><p>Car buying is a bundled transaction. One conversation may combine a vehicle, a trade-in, a loan, insurance-like products, dealer services, taxes, registration, and paperwork. Each part can be legitimate. The risk comes from allowing the bundle to remain opaque. This guide gives you a repeatable way to turn a bundle back into visible, comparable line items.</p><div class="key-takeaways"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul>${p.topics.map(t=>`<li>Ask what <b>${t}</b> means, whether it is required, who receives the money, and where it appears in the contract.</li>`).join('')}<li>Use a preapproval or independent quote as a benchmark, then compare APR and total of payments—not only the payment.</li><li>Do not sign blank fields, accept an unexplained add-on, or take delivery without knowing whether financing is final.</li></ul></div>${svg(p)}</section>
<section class="section"><h2>What buyers are actually paying for</h2><p>The cleanest model is to think in five layers: the vehicle itself; government charges; dealer charges; optional products; and the cost of credit. A fee is not automatically “hidden” because it has a technical name. It becomes a problem when it was not clearly disclosed, is described inaccurately, is treated as mandatory without a basis, duplicates another charge, or is added after the buyer has agreed to a different total.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Examples</th><th>Questions to ask</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Vehicle</td><td>Sale price, market adjustment, accessories, trade allowance</td><td>What is the exact vehicle and price before fees?</td></tr><tr><td>Government</td><td>Sales/use tax, title, registration, plate</td><td>Which amount is set by the state or county?</td></tr><tr><td>Dealer</td><td>Documentation, electronic filing, advertising, preparation</td><td>Is it disclosed, negotiable, and charged consistently?</td></tr><tr><td>Optional</td><td>GAP, service contract, etching, protection, maintenance</td><td>Can I decline it? What are the exclusions and cancellation terms?</td></tr><tr><td>Credit</td><td>APR, finance charge, term, negative equity</td><td>What will I pay in total if every payment is made?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>That distinction matters because the remedy differs. A government charge may need to be paid. A dealer fee may be negotiable. An optional product may be declined or canceled under its contract. A financing term may be improved by comparison shopping. Start with classification before arguing about the dollar amount.</p><h3>${p.angle} in plain English</h3><p>In practical terms, ${p.angle} means identifying the moment a headline offer becomes a real obligation. Read the quote from left to right, then read the contract from top to bottom. Look for changes in the vehicle price, the trade allowance, the payoff, the product selections, the amount financed, the APR, and the number of payments. The “why” of each difference should be explainable in one sentence and visible in writing.</p><div class="protip"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Ask for a clean version with every optional product removed. Then add back only the products you independently choose. This creates a meaningful baseline and makes the cost of each decision visible.</div></section>
<section id="review" class="section"><h2>Step-by-step inspection process</h2><ol><li><b>Get the complete written quote.</b> Request the out-the-door price for the specific stock number or VIN. Do not rely on a verbal payment.</li><li><b>Freeze the vehicle deal.</b> Confirm selling price, rebates, market adjustment, accessories, trade value, trade payoff, and cash down before discussing monthly payment.</li><li><b>Separate required from optional.</b> Mark every product as government-required, lender-required, dealer charge, or optional. Ask for the contractual basis of anything called required.</li><li><b>Run the finance math.</b> Record APR, amount financed, finance charge, term, payment, and total of payments. Recalculate: payment × number of payments, plus any final payment or deferred amount.</li><li><b>Read product terms.</b> For a service contract or GAP product, check term, coverage, exclusions, deductible, cancellation, refund, claims process, and who administers it.</li><li><b>Compare the final copy.</b> Put the quote beside the buyer’s order and retail installment contract. Investigate every mismatch before signing.</li><li><b>Save the record.</b> Photograph or download every page and keep the signed set, receipts, and follow-up promises.</li></ol><div class="callout warning"><strong>Pause signal:</strong> If the dealer says “the bank requires this” or “the payment is the only number that matters,” ask for the requirement in writing and return to the amount financed and total of payments.</div></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Real-world examples</h2><h3>Example 1: the payment that moved</h3><p>A buyer agrees to a $29,000 vehicle at 7.9% for 60 months. In the finance office, a $2,400 service contract and $1,100 GAP product are rolled into the loan. Even before tax and other fees, the buyer is now borrowing $3,500 more and paying interest on those products. The payment may rise only modestly because the term is extended, but the total obligation is materially larger.</p><h3>Example 2: a “small” fee stack</h3><p>A quote shows a $399 documentation charge, a $249 electronic filing charge, a $695 protection package, and a $199 theft-etching charge. None is automatically invalid from the label alone. The buyer’s job is to ask whether each is required, what service it represents, whether it is already included elsewhere, and whether the dealer will remove it. Comparing the total—not debating one fee in isolation—creates leverage.</p><h3>Example 3: trade-in equity changes the picture</h3><p>A trade is worth $16,000 but has a $19,500 payoff. The $3,500 negative equity is added to the new loan. If the buyer then finances optional products, the loan starts farther above the value of the new car. The correct question is not “Can you keep me near $500 a month?” but “What is the exact amount financed and what will I pay in total?”</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Common scams and pressure tactics</h2><ul class="bullets"><li><b>Four-square payment anchoring:</b> the conversation stays on payment while price, trade, and loan term move.</li><li><b>Required-add-on claim:</b> an optional product is described as mandatory without identifying the contract or lender requirement.</li><li><b>Menu fatigue:</b> several products are presented quickly with no time to compare coverage or price.</li><li><b>Back-end surprise:</b> the signed number is higher than the written quote because products or fees were added in the finance office.</li><li><b>Conditional delivery:</b> the vehicle is delivered before the dealer confirms financing is final.</li><li><b>Refund friction:</b> a cancellation request is delayed, redirected, or tied to an in-person visit not clearly required by the product contract.</li></ul><p>A pressure tactic is not proof of illegality, and a dealer may make an honest mistake. Treat the tactic as a reason to slow down, not as a reason to make a public accusation. Write down what was said, request corrected documents, and use the complaint channels listed in the references when the issue is not resolved.</p></section>
<section id="red-flags" class="section"><h2>Red flags and hidden-fee checklist</h2><ul class="checklist"><li>The VIN or stock number does not match across documents</li><li>Vehicle price changes between quote and contract</li><li>“Market adjustment” has no written explanation</li><li>Trade allowance and payoff are not both shown</li><li>Optional products are preselected</li><li>A contract contains blank lines or boxes</li><li>APR or term differs from the approved offer</li><li>Amount financed does not reconcile</li><li>Payment is emphasized without total of payments</li><li>Dealer says financing is pending after delivery</li><li>Cancellation rights are not provided</li><li>Promises are verbal only</li></ul><div class="callout"><strong>Use a three-color mark-up:</strong> green for government charges and terms you understand; yellow for negotiable or optional items; red for unexplained changes, blank fields, or conditional language.</div></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Consumer protection laws: the practical overview</h2><p>Federal and state rules overlap. The Truth in Lending Act and Regulation Z govern many consumer credit disclosures, including the amount financed, finance charge, APR, payment schedule, and total of payments. The Federal Trade Commission enforces rules against deceptive practices and publishes guidance on car add-ons and financing. The CFPB supervises and enforces consumer financial laws within its authority, including issues involving auto lending and add-on products. State attorneys general, motor-vehicle agencies, and finance regulators may regulate dealer licensing, disclosures, fee caps, cancellation rights, and unfair practices.</p><p>Rules vary by state and by the exact document. A legal label such as “doc fee” or “GAP” does not answer whether the charge is fair, negotiable, properly disclosed, or suitable for you. For a dispute, preserve the paperwork, ask the dealer and lender for a written explanation, and consider the CFPB, FTC, state attorney general, or state motor-vehicle agency complaint process. This guide is educational and is not a substitute for advice from a qualified attorney or financial professional.</p><div class="refs"><p><a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-things-can-i-negotiate-when-shopping-for-a-car-or-auto-loan-en-2132/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CFPB: what can be negotiated</a> · <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/financing-or-leasing-car" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FTC: financing or leasing a car</a> · <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/consumer-alerts/2024/08/car-dealerships-cant-charge-you-add-ons-you-dont-want" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FTC: unwanted add-ons</a> · <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-guaranteed-asset-protection-gap-insurance-en-797/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CFPB: GAP</a> · <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-included-in-the-monthly-auto-loan-payment-en-819/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CFPB: payment components</a></p></div></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Statistics and signals worth knowing</h2><div class="stat"><b>75%</b><span>buyers reported add-ons in an FTC 2024 enforcement matter</span></div><div class="stat"><b>5 layers</b><span>to separate in a car deal: vehicle, government, dealer, optional, credit</span></div><div class="stat"><b>1 baseline</b><span>clean quote with optional products removed</span></div><p>Statistics need context. The FTC’s 2024 consumer alert reported that as many as 75% of Asbury Automotive buyers in the cited matter said add-ons were tacked onto the sales contract secretly or by being described as required. That is an enforcement-related figure, not a universal rate for every dealer. The more durable takeaway is process-based: optional products should be clearly identified and voluntarily selected, and the buyer should receive a contract that matches the agreement.</p></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Negotiation tips and money-saving checklist</h2><p>Negotiate in this order: vehicle price, trade value and payoff, financing offer, optional products, then the final arithmetic. Ask for the out-the-door total in writing before visiting. Bring a preapproval, but do not assume it is automatically the cheapest—compare APR, fees, term, and total of payments. When a dealer says a fee is non-negotiable, you can still compare its total with another dealer’s total or ask for an equivalent discount elsewhere.</p><ul class="checklist"><li>Request an out-the-door quote by email</li><li>Get a bank or credit-union preapproval</li><li>Price insurance and GAP independently</li><li>Ask for a clean quote without add-ons</li><li>Negotiate the total, not the payment</li><li>Keep trade and new-car numbers separate</li><li>Ask for every promise in writing</li><li>Take time before signing</li><li>Decline any product you do not understand</li><li>Recheck the final signed copy at home</li></ul></section>
<section id="checklist" class="section"><div class="printable"><h2>Printable checklist</h2><h3>Before I sign, I verified:</h3><ul class="checklist"><li>Vehicle VIN, mileage, trim, and condition</li><li>Agreed vehicle price and all discounts</li><li>Every dealer and government charge</li><li>Trade value, payoff, and net equity</li><li>Optional product selection and price</li><li>APR, term, payment, amount financed</li><li>Finance charge and total of payments</li><li>Whether delivery and financing are final</li><li>My copy of every signed document</li><li>Who to contact with a correction</li></ul><p class="disclaimer">Print this page for personal use. Confirm state-specific requirements with the relevant agency.</p></div></section>
<section id="faq" class="section"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>${faqHtml}</section>
<section class="section"><h2>Myth vs. fact</h2><div class="myth"><div><b>Myth</b>“The dealer says it is required, so I have no choice.”</div><div><b>Fact</b>Ask whether it is required by law, by the lender, or simply included by the dealer. Those are different claims.</div><div><b>Myth</b>“The payment is low, so the deal is affordable.”</div><div><b>Fact</b>A longer term can lower the payment while increasing total interest and keeping you upside down longer.</div><div><b>Myth</b>“A fee is hidden only if it is illegal.”</div><div><b>Fact</b>Hidden can describe poor visibility or surprise. Legality and value are separate questions.</div><div><b>Myth</b>“I can fix the paperwork later.”</div><div><b>Fact</b>It is easier to correct a document before signing than to unwind a financed transaction later.</div></div></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Glossary</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><tbody>${[['Amount financed','The amount of credit provided for the transaction after the disclosed adjustments.'],['APR','The annualized cost of credit, including interest and certain finance charges.'],['Finance charge','The dollar cost of credit disclosed in the lending documents.'],['GAP','An optional product intended to address some difference between loan balance and insurance settlement after a covered total loss.'],['Out-the-door price','The complete purchase total including applicable dealer, government, and selected product charges.'],['Negative equity','The amount by which a trade payoff exceeds its value.'],['Service contract','A separate repair-coverage agreement with its own exclusions and claim rules.'],['Dealer-arranged financing','Credit obtained through the dealer from a third-party lender.']].map(x=>`<tr><td><b>${x[0]}</b></td><td>${x[1]}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div></section>
<section class="section"><h2>Related resources</h2><div class="related">${other.map(x=>`<a href="/${x.slug}">${x.title}</a>`).join('')}</div><div class="cta-box"><h2>Turn a stack of paperwork into a decision</h2><p>HiddenFeeAI reviews quotes, purchase agreements, loan documents, and add-on terms to surface hidden fees, financing risks, unfair contract language, and negotiation opportunities. Upload a document when you want a second set of eyes before the next signature.</p><a class="cta" href="${ai}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Review my car documents with HiddenFeeAI ↗</a></div></section>${extra}
</article><aside class="toc"><strong>On this page</strong><a href="#summary">Executive summary</a><a href="#review">Inspection process</a><a href="#red-flags">Red flags</a><a href="#checklist">Printable checklist</a><a href="#faq">FAQs</a><a href="${ai}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Analyze with HiddenFeeAI ↗</a></aside></div></div></main>${sharedFooter}`;
}

function page(p){ const faq = Array.from({length:20},(_,i)=>i); const faqHtml=faqItems(p); const desc=p.short.slice(0,155); return `<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>${esc(p.title)} | DetectHiddenFees</title><meta name="description" content="${esc(desc)}"><link rel="canonical" href="${site}/${p.slug}"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow"><meta property="og:type" content="article"><meta property="og:title" content="${esc(p.title)} | DetectHiddenFees"><meta property="og:description" content="${esc(desc)}"><meta property="og:url" content="${site}/${p.slug}"><meta property="og:image" content="${site}/og-image.png"><meta property="og:site_name" content="DetectHiddenFees"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:title" content="${esc(p.title)} | DetectHiddenFees"><meta name="twitter:description" content="${esc(desc)}"><meta name="twitter:image" content="${site}/og-image.png"><link rel="alternate" type="application/rss+xml" href="/rss.xml" title="DetectHiddenFees updates"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/phase1-foundation.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/automotive-authority.css"><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;500;700;800;900&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">${schema(p,faq)}${commonCss}</head><body><a class="phase1-skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to main content</a><div class="orb-accent"></div>${body(p,faqHtml)}</body></html>`; }

function hub(){ const cards=pages.map((p,i)=>`<a class="hub-card" href="/${p.slug}"><span>${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')} · ${p.tag}</span><h3>${p.title}</h3><p>${p.short}</p><b>Read the field guide →</b></a>`).join(''); return `<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Car Dealer Fees &amp; Auto Financing Authority Hub | DetectHiddenFees</title><meta name="description" content="The definitive consumer guide to car dealer fees, auto loan fine print, add-ons, GAP, dealer markup, spot delivery, contracts, and negotiation."><link rel="canonical" href="${site}/car-dealer-fees"><meta property="og:title" content="Car Dealer Fees & Auto Financing Authority Hub"><meta property="og:description" content="15 evidence-led guides for understanding the real cost of buying and financing a car."><meta property="og:url" content="${site}/car-dealer-fees"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><link rel="alternate" type="application/rss+xml" href="/rss.xml">${commonCss}<style>.hub-hero{padding:92px 0 58px}.hub-hero h1{font-size:clamp(3rem,7vw,6.6rem);line-height:.95;letter-spacing:-.08em;max-width:950px}.hub-hero h1 em{font-style:normal;color:#8edcff}.hub-hero p{font-size:1.2rem;max-width:760px}.hub-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:15px}.hub-card{display:block;text-decoration:none;color:inherit;padding:24px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:20px;background:linear-gradient(150deg,rgba(14,31,65,.85),rgba(8,17,36,.72));transition:.25s}.hub-card:hover{transform:translateY(-4px);border-color:rgba(56,189,248,.5)}.hub-card span{font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.12em;font-weight:900;color:#8edcff}.hub-card h3{font-size:1.2rem;margin:16px 0 10px}.hub-card p{font-size:.92rem;color:#b6c5dc}.hub-card b{font-size:.84rem;color:#b9e7ff}@media(max-width:900px){.hub-grid{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}}@media(max-width:600px){.hub-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}}</style></head><body><header><div class="wrap nav"><a class="logo" href="/">Detect<span>HiddenFees</span></a><nav class="navlinks"><a href="/hidden-auto-fees">Auto fees overview</a><a class="navcta" href="${ai}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Analyze a document ↗</a></nav></div></header><main><div class="wrap"><section class="hub-hero"><div class="eyebrow">THE AUTOMOTIVE AUTHORITY HUB · ${today}</div><h1>See the deal <em>clearly.</em></h1><p>The complete consumer library for car dealer fees, auto financing, purchase contracts, add-ons, and negotiation. Built to help you understand the real cost before you sign.</p><div class="answer-first"><strong>Start here:</strong> Ask for the out-the-door price, separate optional products from required charges, and compare APR and total of payments—not only the monthly payment.</div></section><section class="section"><h2>Choose your next question</h2><div class="hub-grid">${cards}</div></section><section class="section"><h2>How to use this hub</h2><p>Start with the guide closest to the document or conversation in front of you. Use the checklists at the end of each article to mark up the quote, buyer’s order, retail installment contract, or add-on agreement. When you have the paperwork, HiddenFeeAI can help you surface mismatches, hidden fees, renewal language, financing risks, and terms worth negotiating.</p>${svg(pages[0])}<div class="cta-box"><h2>Have the paperwork already?</h2><p>Upload it to HiddenFeeAI for a structured review of fees, add-ons, finance terms, and negotiation opportunities.</p><a class="cta" href="${ai}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Analyze my documents ↗</a></div></section></div></main><footer><div class="wrap">DetectHiddenFees · Car Dealer Fee Authority Hub</div></footer></body></html>`; }

function normalizeHub(html){ return html.replace(/<body><header[\s\S]*?<\/header>/, `<body><a class="phase1-skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to main content</a><div class="orb-accent"></div>${sharedHeader}`).replace(/<footer>[\s\S]*?<\/footer><\/body>/, `${sharedFooter}</body>`).replace('<main>','<main id="main-content" class="article-page"><nav class="phase2-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="phase2-separator" aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Car Dealer Fee Authority Hub</span></nav>'); }
function personalizeBenchmarks(html,p){ let i=0; html=html.replaceAll('Next, compare the item with an independent benchmark.',()=>`Next, compare ${p.topics[i++%p.topics.length]} with an independent benchmark.`); let j=0; html=html.replaceAll('<b>Apply it here:</b>',()=>`<b>Apply it here at checkpoint ${++j}:</b>`); return html.replace('<div class="cta-box"><h2>Turn a stack of paperwork into a decision</h2>','<div class="related"><a href="/ai-construction-contract-review">Construction contract fee review</a><a href="/ai-contract-review-software">SaaS and software contract review</a><a href="/ai-contract-review">Contract review authority hub</a><a href="/hidden-fee-industry-guide">Hidden fee industry guide</a></div><div class="cta-box"><h2>Turn a stack of paperwork into a decision</h2>'); }
function renderHtml(html){ let out=html.replace(commonCss,'').replaceAll('href="/phase1-foundation.css"','href="phase1-foundation.css"').replaceAll('href="/automotive-authority.css"','href="automotive-authority.css?v=293116e"').replaceAll('href="automotive-authority.css"','href="automotive-authority.css?v=293116e"'); if(!out.includes('automotive-authority.css')) out=out.replace('</head>', `<link rel="stylesheet" href="phase1-foundation.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="automotive-authority.css?v=293116e"></head>`); return out; }
function optimizeHtml(out){
  const hubPage=out.includes('<title>Car Dealer Fees &amp; Auto Financing Authority Hub');
  const hubFaq=[['What is the fastest way to use the automotive hub?','Start with the guide that matches the document or conversation in front of you, then compare the written out-the-door total, APR, amount financed, and total of payments.'],['Are dealer add-ons required?','Many dealer add-ons are optional. Ask the dealer to identify any claimed legal or lender requirement in writing and request a clean quote without optional products.'],['What should I review before signing?','Review the vehicle price, every fee, trade value and payoff, selected products, APR, term, payment, amount financed, finance charge, total of payments, and whether delivery is final.'],['Can HiddenFeeAI review car-buying documents?','HiddenFeeAI can help surface fees, add-ons, financing risks, cancellation language, mismatched numbers, and negotiation opportunities in uploaded documents.'],['What if a dealer changes the terms after delivery?','Do not sign a replacement contract without comparing it line by line with the original. Preserve every document and ask for the financing condition and deadline in writing.']];
  if(hubPage){
    if(!out.includes('id="hub-faq"')) out=out.replace('</main>',`<section id="hub-faq" class="section"><h2>Automotive hub FAQs</h2>${hubFaq.map(x=>`<details><summary>${x[0]}</summary><p>${x[1]}</p></details>`).join('')}</section></main>`);
    if(!out.includes('"@type":"FAQPage"')) out=out.replace('</head>',`<script type="application/ld+json">${JSON.stringify({"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage",mainEntity:hubFaq.map(x=>({"@type":"Question",name:x[0],acceptedAnswer:{"@type":"Answer",text:x[1]}}))})}</script></head>`);
  }
  const fontHref='https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;500;700;800;900&display=swap';
  const fontLink=`<link rel="preload" as="style" href="${fontHref}"><link href="${fontHref}" rel="stylesheet" media="print" onload="this.media='all'"><noscript><link href="${fontHref}" rel="stylesheet"></noscript>`;
  out=out.replace(`<link href="${fontHref}" rel="stylesheet">`,fontLink);
  if(!out.includes('rel="icon"')) out=out.replace('</head>','<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="favicon.svg"><link rel="alternate icon" href="favicon.svg"><link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="favicon.svg"></head>');
  if(!out.includes('name="robots"')) out=out.replace('</head>','<meta name="robots" content="index,follow"></head>');
  if(!out.includes('name="twitter:card"')) out=out.replace('</head>','<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:title" content="DetectHiddenFees Automotive Authority Hub"><meta name="twitter:description" content="Evidence-led guides to car dealer fees, auto financing, contracts, add-ons, and negotiation."></head>');
  if(!out.includes('"@type":"Article"')) out=out.replace('</head>',`<script type="application/ld+json">${JSON.stringify({"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article",headline:'Car Dealer Fees & Auto Financing Authority Hub',description:'The definitive consumer guide to car dealer fees, auto loan fine print, add-ons, GAP, dealer markup, spot delivery, contracts, and negotiation.',author:{"@type":'Organization',name:'DetectHiddenFees Research Team'},publisher:{"@type":'Organization',name:'DetectHiddenFees',url:site},datePublished:today,dateModified:today,mainEntityOfPage:{"@type":'WebPage',"@id":`${site}/car-dealer-fees`}})}</script></head>`);
  if(!out.includes('"@type":"BreadcrumbList"')) out=out.replace('</head>',`<script type="application/ld+json">${JSON.stringify({"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList",itemListElement:[{"@type":'ListItem',position:1,name:'Home',item:site},{"@type":'ListItem',position:2,name:'Car Dealer Fee Authority Hub',item:`${site}/car-dealer-fees`}]})}</script></head>`);
  if(!out.includes('"@type":"SpeakableSpecification"')) out=out.replace('</head>',`<script type="application/ld+json">${JSON.stringify({"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"SpeakableSpecification",cssSelector:['.answer-first','.key-takeaways']})}</script></head>`);
  const deepStart=out.indexOf('<section class="section"><h2>Deep dive:');
  const summaryStart=out.indexOf('<section id="summary"');
  const deepAnchor='<section class="section"><h2>What buyers are actually paying for</h2>';
  if(deepStart>=0 && summaryStart>deepStart && out.includes(deepAnchor)){
    const deep=out.slice(deepStart,summaryStart);
    out=out.slice(0,deepStart)+out.slice(summaryStart);
    out=out.replace(deepAnchor,deep+deepAnchor);
  }
  return out;
}
